(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110200-a-moving-defcid-reaches-the-conformance-corpus-and-no-deployment-lock")
  (:adr/topic "gap-02-defcid-blast-radius")
  (:adr/title "A moving DefCID reaches the language conformance corpus and no deployment lock")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/osaho"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110100-payload-v3-has-no-representation-blocker-only-a-conversion-contract"
                "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

This is the second half of gap 02. The first half established that payload v3 is
POSSIBLE: every tag osaho's normalize emits round-trips through kotoba.value.v1,
so nothing waits on io-ipld. What remained was whether v3 is AFFORDABLE, and
that is a question about how far a moving DefCID reaches.

## Measurement

A DefCID is `mf/cidv1-dag-cbor` of the canonical typed KIR, so on disk it is a
`bafyrei...` base32 string. Counting that PREFIX would be the wrong measurement:
every dag-cbor CID shares it, so app CIDs and graph CIDs would be counted as
DefCIDs. The discriminator is the KEY -- `:definition-cid` and
`:dep/definition-cids`.

    find orgs -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 10 \\( -name '*.edn' -o -name '*.kotoba' \\) \\
      -not -path '*/node_modules/*' -not -path '*/.git/*' -print0 \\
      | xargs -0 grep -lE ':(dep/)?definition-cids?\\s'

## Result: the reach is the conformance corpus, and it stops there

70 files pin a DefCID key, across nine repositories once `lang-pure-wt` is
recognised as a worktree duplicate of `kotoba-lang` rather than a tenth. They
sit in `lang/code-identity-conformance/{negative,positive}`,
`lang/package-conformance/{negative,positive}`, `lang/`,
`resources/kotoba/lang/` and `resources/kotoba/lang/wasm-packages`.

**Zero of the 70 is a deployment lock, an app manifest, or a capability
manifest.** That was checked directly rather than inferred: a separate scan
found 608 such files (523 kotoba.app.edn, 61 capability.edn, 22 kotoba.lock.edn,
2 sources.lock.edn) and none of them names a DefCID key, while the same grep
FIRES on osaho's frozen vectors and extracts exactly its ten. A zero that has
not been controlled is not a measurement, and this one is.

Two of the filenames are `contract-lock.edn` and
`invalid_definition_cid_lock.edn`. They are named lock and they are NOT locks --
they are conformance fixtures under `lang/package-conformance/`. The lock reader
in osaho does support `:dep/definition-cids`, so the FORMAT can pin a DefCID;
what the measurement says is that no lock on disk currently does.

## The count is a range, and the range is the honest answer

13 distinct DefCIDs sit on a `definition-cid` line. 25 distinct CID literals
appear in the files that pin DefCIDs. The gap is not noise: `:dep/definition-cids`
is a VECTOR and may span lines, so a same-line extractor cannot see its
continuations. The control -- the same extractor against the frozen vectors --
yields exactly ten, which is the number that file pins.

So the pinned population is between 13 and 25 and the difference is multi-line
vectors. Reporting one of those two as the count would be presenting an
artefact of the regex as a property of the corpus. It does not change the
decision, so it is left as a range.

Separately, the three copies of `code-identity-vectors.edn` -- the authority in
kotoba-lang, the copy in osaho, and the one in the lang-pure-wt worktree -- pin
ten DefCIDs each and agree on the identical set (one shasum across all three).

## Consequence: v3 is affordable, and the reason is structural

A payload v3 moves every DefCID. The measurement says what that invalidates: a
conformance corpus that exists in order to be regenerated. The vectors file
states its own regeneration rule -- a changed identity means either a real
semantic change, in which case bump `:payload-version` and regenerate, or a bug.
v3 is exactly the first case.

Nothing deployed pins a DefCID today, so v3 does not invalidate a lock anyone is
running. Combined with the first half, both objections to v3 are now answered by
measurement rather than by argument: it is not blocked upstream, and it does not
break deployments.

**This ADR still does not promote io-ipld to :deps.** It removes the second of
the two reasons for hesitating. The remaining work is the conversion contract
itself -- the tag alphabet, the number wrappers, the map ordering -- which is
where the design decisions actually are.

## A method note worth keeping

The first version of the file scan used `-maxdepth 6`, and it silently missed
osaho's own copy of the frozen vectors at depth 7. The undercount was caught only
because a narrower scan for one known filename returned two copies where three
were expected. Re-run at depth 10 the DefCID site count went from 56 to 70: the
depth limit had been hiding a fifth of the answer, and nothing in the output said
so. A bounded scan reports its bound as if it were the world.")))
